@node clawtilla Notifications
@subsection Notifications, mutes and read marks

The notification list is the daemon's.  @code{notice.list} answers four
groups --- conversations with something unread, decisions waiting, Grok
jobs that need somebody, and alerts --- already less the agents you have
muted, and every client draws the same list.
@code{cmacs-clawtilla-alerts} is that list.

@table @kbd
@item RET
Go where the row leads: the conversation, the Decisions page, the Grok
page.
@item r
Mark read: one alert (@code{notice.read}), or the whole conversation
(@code{room.read}).
@item d
Dismiss an alert (@code{notice.dismiss}); on a Grok job, remove it after
asking (@code{grok.remove}).
@item z
Mute the row's agent, or unmute it (@code{notice.mute},
@code{notice.unmute}).
@item U
Unmute an agent, chosen from those muted.
@item a
Narrow the buffer to the row's agent, or widen it again.
@item m
Mark everything read: every alert and every conversation.
@item x
Clear the alerts (@code{notice.clear}), or only the narrowed agent's.
@item o
Load fifty lines of the daemon's event log.
@end table

Opening the buffer reads the alerts, as the GTK panel does on opening.
An alert that arrives while the buffer is on screen is read at once ---
libclawt's @code{clawt_alert_arrives_read} --- because otherwise the bell
counts a row you watched appear.

@subsubheading The bell

The number is @code{clawt_notice_list_badge}, computed by the library
from the daemon's reply, so every client shows the same one.  It is in
the mode line of every clawtilla buffer
(@code{cmacs-clawtilla-mode-line-bell}) and in the fleet buffer's first
line.

@subsubheading Unread counts

The daemon holds the read marks.  A count is therefore the daemon's
number, taken in by @code{clawt_unread_seed} whenever the rooms are
listed --- which is why a client that has just connected shows what
everybody else does.  Between listings a message raises a count by
@code{clawt_unread_should_count}, and a @code{room.read} event --- from
this client, another one, or the CLI --- sets it to the daemon's number
again.

Opening a conversation marks it read @emph{up to the last message
drawn}, after the history has been drawn and never before: the history's
@code{read_upto} is what places the ``New messages'' rule
(@code{clawt_unread_rule_index}), above the first message somebody else
sent after the mark.  A transcript reloading in a buried buffer marks
nothing read.

@subsubheading Mutes

A mute is the daemon's too, and holds for every client.  A muted agent's
row keeps its count, dimmed: what a mute takes away is the interruption,
so the conversation leaves every total (@code{clawt_unread_total}) and
stays out of the bell.

@subsubheading A bug this fixed

The fleet buffer set @code{cmacs-clawtilla-known-rooms-function} to a
function that called @code{cmacs-clawtilla-known-rooms}, which calls
whatever that variable holds.  The recursion was unbounded and happened
inside the event dispatch, where errors are swallowed by design --- so
with a fleet buffer open no unread count ever rose, and nothing said
why.

@node clawtilla Tasks and Projects
@subsection Tasks, broken runs and projects

@subsubheading The Tasks page

Broken runs first, then every task.

A broken run is an @dfn{incident}: a run that failed, stalled or could
not start, or a routine that did not run.  The daemon reports it to
somebody; @kbd{c} changes whether it does and to whom
(@code{incident.configure}, the recipients being libclawt's
@samp{incident-recipient} family).  @kbd{r} on an incident that can be
retried retries it.

Each task shows its state as a badge in the tone libclawt gives that
state, who asked for it and who has it, its project, and --- in the
library's words --- what it is waiting on.

@table @kbd
@item i
New task (@code{task.create}): what, an optional title, who, what it
waits for, a repository for a checkout of its own.
@item RET
The task's own page.
@item a
Assign it (@code{task.assign}).
@item r
Retry the task, or the broken run, at point (@code{task.retry}).
@item D
Cancel it (@code{task.cancel}).
@item o
Open the conversation it is being worked in.
@item c
How broken runs are reported.
@item J
Projects.
@end table

Every task frame names its task as @code{task}.  An earlier version of
this client sent @code{id} to @code{task.cancel}, which the daemon does
not read.

@subsubheading One task

@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-task} (@code{task.show}, with its journal).
What was asked; the result; the thread of notes; what it waits for; its
checkout; its VENTURE ticket; its review; its history.

@table @kbd
@item c
Comment (@code{task.comment}); the assignee hears it while the task is
with them.
@item a
Assign.
@item u
@itemx U
Make it wait for another task, or stop (@code{task.link},
@code{task.unlink}).
@item d
Show what its checkout changed (@code{task.diff}), in @code{diff-mode}.
@item X
Remove the checkout; the branch is kept (@code{task.checkout_remove}).
@item v
Your verdict on a result in review: accept, or ask for changes
(@code{task.review}).
@item o
Open its ticket, else its conversation.
@item V
Send it to VENTURE again (@code{task.venture_retry}).
@item r
@itemx D
Retry, cancel.
@end table

@subsubheading Projects

@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-projects}.  A project says what work is for and
keeps a monthly budget on it.  @kbd{i} makes one (@code{project.create};
the id cannot be changed afterwards), @kbd{RET} changes a field
(@code{project.set} --- name, goal, directory, who reviews, which
agents, the budget, the warning percent, and whether work is refused
once the budget is spent), @kbd{D} removes it (@code{project.remove}).
Who reviews is libclawt's @samp{review-by} family, worded for a project,
or any agent by id.

@node clawtilla Conversation Files
@subsection Files sent in a conversation

@kbd{P} in a conversation (@code{attachment.list}): every file sent in
it, by you or the agent, with a preview --- the picture itself for an
image, the first page for a PDF and the first lines for a text file, the
last two being the daemon's work (@code{attachment.thumbnail}).
@kbd{RET} opens a file, @kbd{s} saves a copy, @kbd{y} copies a local
path.

Fetched bytes are cached under
@file{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/clawtilla/attachments} by id --- the GTK client's
own cache, so a file one client fetched is there for the other.  An id
is reduced to a file name first, so one with a slash in it cannot name a
file outside the cache.  Only the first
@code{cmacs-clawtilla-files-previews} files get a preview, each a
request.

@kbd{a} in a conversation sends files @emph{with} a message.  Each goes
to the daemon first (@code{attachment.put}) and the message names them
in libclawt's attachment block (@code{clawt_attachment_block_new}) ---
the one spelling every client previews from and @code{attachment.list}
finds.  A file the daemon refused is listed as not saved rather than
dropped.

@node clawtilla Devices and Pairing
@subsection Devices and pairing

@subsubheading From the daemon's side

@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-devices} (@code{device.list}): who is paired,
when each was last seen, and @kbd{D} to revoke one or sign this one out
(@code{device.revoke}).  Whether a row offers that at all is libclawt's
rule: only the owner revokes somebody else.

@kbd{i} asks the daemon for a six-digit code (@code{device.pair}) and
shows it, grouped for reading by the library, with a countdown and a QR
code of the pairing link.  The QR code is an SVG where this Emacs draws
images, libclawt's half-block rendering where it does not, and the link
itself when libclawt was built without a QR encoder.  Closing the buffer
before the code is used cancels it (@code{device.pair_cancel}); the
daemon's @code{device.paired} event ends the countdown.

@subsubheading From this machine's side

@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-pair} takes a daemon's address and the code
somebody read off the other screen, trades it for a token of this
machine's own, saves that as a profile in clawtilla's connections file,
and connects.  The code's shape is checked by the library before
anything is dialled, so a typo costs nothing against the daemon's guess
limit.

The exchange blocks, so it does not run on Emacs's thread --- which
under @option{--gowl} is the compositor's.  It runs on a GLib worker and
its outcome is delivered on the cmacs GLib context like any other reply.

@subsubheading The daemon you were last on

@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-resume}, which is also what @kbd{M-x
cmacs-clawtilla} does with nothing connected.  Which daemon that is
comes from @code{clawt_connection_choose}: the saved profile this client
was last switched to, then the local daemon.  The record is a name in a
file every clawtilla client shares, never an address or a token.
Connecting to a saved profile writes it; connecting to the local daemon
removes it.  Set @code{cmacs-clawtilla-resume-last} to @code{nil} to be
asked which daemon instead.

A remembered daemon that never answers is described as the one you were
last on --- @code{clawt_connection_notice_text} now receives the real
``remembered'' flag --- and @kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-use-local} is the
way out, offered when @code{clawt_connection_offer_local} says so.

@node clawtilla Conversation Marks
@subsection A conversation

@table @asis
@item Marks
A message folded into a running turn, cut short, acknowledged without
waking anybody, a consultation, a cross-talk summary: each is marked on
the message's heading in libclawt's words.
@item Folding
Another agent's long message to the agent whose conversation this is
folds to one line (@code{clawt_chat_should_fold}); @kbd{TAB} opens it.
@item Rounds
In a group room a new round is labelled where it starts, and somebody
named who had already answered is noted as not delivered, with the
reason.
@item Decisions
A decision asked in the conversation is drawn at its live edge with its
options; @kbd{y} answers it (@code{decision.answer}, recorded as
answered from the chat).
@item Rescue
A turn that has said nothing for a while is offered
(@code{turn.rescue}): @kbd{u} stops it, or sends the same message again
on another model for this turn only.
@item Apps
A tool call that came with an MCP app carries a link; @kbd{o} on its
line opens it (@code{connector.app_get}).
@item Links
@kbd{M-x cmacs-clawtilla-open} follows a @samp{clawtilla://} link, or a
bare agent or room name, to the conversation it names
(@code{clawt_open_target_resolve}).
@end table

