@node clawtilla Pushed Events
@subsection Pushed events

The daemon pushes; nothing here polls.
@code{cmacs-clawtilla-events-table} lists every pushed event that no
buffer is named after, with what it moves:

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .62
@headitem Event @tab Moves
@item @code{agent.changed} @tab the agent's page
@item @code{agent.state} @tab Fleet and Activity pages, the agent's page
@item @code{agent.idle_stopped}
@tab Fleet page, the agent's page; said once
@item @code{agent.failover}
@tab Fleet page, the agent's page; said once
@item @code{broker.changed}
@tab the broker page, Settings' Connectors panel
@item @code{decision.asked}, @code{decision.deleted}
@tab Decisions, the proposals page, the conversation it was asked in
@item @code{device.paired} @tab the Devices page, the pairing buffer
@item @code{image.progress}, @code{image.finished}
@tab Settings' Cloud images, with how far a download has got
@item @code{memory.changed} @tab the Memory page and the memory review
@item @code{notice.changed} @tab the bell and the notifications buffer
@item @code{routine.turned_off}
@tab Routines; said once, in the library's words
@item @code{sentinel.finished} @tab Issues
@item @code{steps.replayed}
@tab Saved steps; says how the replay ended
@item @code{task.tree_budget}
@tab Tasks; said once, in the library's words
@item @code{turn.no_reply} @tab the conversation's rescue offer
@item @code{turn.steered} @tab the conversation, Activity
@end multitable

Nothing is patched from an event.  An event says what changed, not what
everything now is, so each of these asks again, coalesced so that a
burst is one request.

@node clawtilla Page Framework
@subsection One buffer kind for a list and its verbs

Most of the above is the same thing many times: ask the daemon for a
list, draw a row per entry with a line of detail under it, offer three
or four verbs.  @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-clawtilla-page.el} is that, once.

@lisp
(cmacs-clawtilla-define-page "waits"
  :title "Waiting for"
  :needs-agent t
  :fetch '((waits "wait.list"))
  :draw #'cmacs-clawtilla-waits--draw
  :events "\\`wait\\."
  :verbs '(("D" "cancel this wait" cmacs-clawtilla-waits-cancel)))
@end lisp

The framework fetches every frame of a page at once and keeps each
answer twice --- parsed, for drawing, and as the text the daemon sent,
for libclawt --- redraws keeping point on its row, reloads (coalesced)
on the events the page names, and builds the page's transient from its
verbs.  A frame that failed is said on the page.

The keys are the part that is not obvious.  A clawtilla buffer is an
Evil intercept map, promoted per major mode, so a keymap per page would
be a mode per page.  Instead the one mode map binds every letter that is
safe to take in a read-only buffer to a dispatcher that runs whichever
verb the current page gave that key.  A page therefore cannot bind a
motion key even by mistake.

@node clawtilla Asking the Library
@subsection Asking the library

Nearly every sentence above is libclawt's.  0.52 words a great deal more
than 0.11 did, and each helper needs the same thing from this side ---
some values in, a string out --- so there is one primitive rather than
fifty:

@lisp
(cmacs-clawtilla-ask "device-seen" :last_seen 1763650000 :connected :false)
;; => "seen 1 hour ago"
(cmacs-clawtilla-ask-parsed "task-state" :state "running")
;; => ((tone . "info") (retryable) (cancellable . t))
@end lisp

@code{cmacs-clawtilla--ask} takes the name of a question and its
arguments as a JSON object; the table of questions is in
@file{cmacs/clawtilla/cmacs-clawtilla-render.c} and
@code{cmacs-clawtilla--ask-names} lists it.  A value the daemon sent
must travel as the text it sent, not re-encoded from the parsed form ---
a null re-encoded from an alist is an empty object, which the library
reads as a value --- so such a value is passed as @code{(:json . TEXT)},
and @code{cmacs-clawtilla-request-both} exists to give a caller both
halves of a reply.

The choice lists are asked for in the same spirit, through
@code{cmacs-clawtilla-enum}: @samp{effort}, @samp{weekday},
@samp{schedule}, @samp{resolution}, @samp{usage-group},
@samp{ack-check-model}, @samp{review-by}, @samp{incident-recipient},
@samp{steer-policy} and @samp{task-state} joined the families that were
already walked.

@node clawtilla Not Built
@subsection What is not here

Stated rather than left to be found:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The GTK client's Appearance fonts and its window chrome have no Emacs
equivalent to build.
@item
Model fallback lists, the per-agent network (egress) choice and the
confine switch have no row on an agent's page.
@item
A connector's tool-directory mode, how a computer starts, a recording's
source, and a trigger's mode and provider are typed rather than chosen
from libclawt's lists.
@item
Mention completion, per-conversation drafts, and the split view of a
merged delivery are not built.
@item
The cross-talk summary setting and the ``view exchange'' window for a
folded agent-to-agent message are not built; the fold itself and the
cross-talk mark are.
@end itemize

